.TH OPENCONNECT-SAML 1 "2026-10-12" "openconnect-saml 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
openconnect-saml \- connect to a Cisco AnyConnect VPN that authenticates with SAML/SSO
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B openconnect-saml
.I command
.RI [ profile ]
.RI [ options ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B openconnect-saml
drives the SAML/SSO login of a Cisco AnyConnect gateway and hands the resulting
session cookie to
.BR openconnect (8).
Your ordinary desktop browser performs the login, so hardware keys, push
notifications, password managers and existing sessions behave exactly as they do
elsewhere. No embedded webview, no browser-automation stack.
.PP
Cisco gateways offer SAML in one of two shapes, and
.B connect
asks the gateway which it speaks before choosing. See
.B AUTHENTICATION MODES
below.
.PP
Authentication runs as the invoking user; only the tunnel itself is started with
elevated privilege, so the browser opens in your own desktop session.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B connect
Authenticate, then start the tunnel in the background. Returns once the tunnel
is up, because
.BR openconnect (8)
only daemonises after the connection is established.
.TP
.B cookie
Authenticate and print
.BR COOKIE ,
.B CONNECT_URL
and
.B FINGERPRINT
as shell-quoted assignments, without connecting. Suitable for
.BR eval .
.TP
.B probe
Ask the gateway how it authenticates and report what it says: the resolved
tunnel group, the login URL, the token cookie name, the browser mode, and
whether the local
.BR openconnect (8)
can drive it.
.TP
.B status
Report whether the tunnel is up. Exits 0 when it is, 1 when it is not.
.TP
.B disconnect
Stop a tunnel started by
.BR connect .
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-s " URL" "\fR, \fP" \-\-server " URL"
The gateway URL,
.I including the group path
- for example
.IR https://vpn.example.com/saml .
On a Cisco gateway the URL path is what selects the tunnel group. Required
unless a profile supplies it.
.TP
.BI \-g " GROUP" "\fR, \fP" \-\-group " GROUP"
Send
.I GROUP
as
.BR <group-select> .
Most gateways ignore this; prefer the URL path. See
.BR "TUNNEL GROUPS" .
.TP
.BI \-b " CMD" "\fR, \fP" \-\-browser " CMD"
Command that opens a URL, taking it as the first argument. A Chromium-based
command (chromium, chrome, brave, vivaldi, edge) also enables automatic cookie
capture. Use
.RB \(dq \- \(dq
to launch nothing and only print the URL.
.TP
.BI \-\-sudo " CMD"
Privilege helper used for the tunnel. Empty to run
.BR openconnect (8)
directly. Default
.BR sudo .
.TP
.BI \-m " MODE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-mode " MODE"
Authentication route:
.BR auto ", " external ", " token " or " browserless .
Default
.BR auto .
.TP
.BI \-\-capture " HOW"
How
.B token
mode collects the cookie:
.B auto
(the DevTools protocol when the browser supports it, else a prompt),
.B cdp
(insist on the protocol) or
.B paste
(always prompt). Default
.BR auto .
.TP
.BI \-u " NAME" "\fR, \fP" \-\-user " NAME"
Username for
.B browserless
mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-password\-command " CMD"
Command whose first line of output is the password, for
.B browserless
mode - for example
.RB \(dq "pass show vpn/work" \(dq.
.TP
.BI \-\-factor " NAME"
Which second factor to take when the identity provider offers a choice as an
HTML form - several submit buttons, or a radio group.
.I NAME
is matched case-insensitively, as a substring, against each control's
.BR name ", " value " and " id ,
so
.B \-\-factor push
finds
.IR value=\(dqDuo\ Push\(dq ,
.I id=push-button
and
.I name=push
alike. Without it the first submit control is taken, as a browser does when you
press Enter; a form offering a real choice with nothing matching
.I NAME
is an error listing what is on offer, rather than a guess. Has no effect on a
second factor drawn by JavaScript, such as Duo's prompt, where the choice is
made in the browser.
.TP
.BI \-\-debug\-dir " DIR"
Save each page of a
.B browserless
login into
.IR DIR .
These pages contain CSRF tokens and SAML assertions, so
.I DIR
is created mode 0700 and the pages 0600.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-print
Print the
.BR openconnect (8)
command instead of running it.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-insecure
Skip TLS verification while authenticating, and allow a plain
.B http://
gateway. Does not affect the tunnel, which is pinned separately. Exposes the SSO
token and the session cookie to anyone on the path; for a private-CA gateway,
not for convenience. Refused with
.BR "\-\-mode browserless" ,
which submits a password rather than a token.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Narrate the exchange on standard error.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help. Accepted by every subcommand.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.
.SH AUTHENTICATION MODES
.TP
.B external
The gateway is configured with
.IR "saml external-browser" .
.BR openconnect (8)
implements this natively, listening on localhost port 29786 for a token
encrypted to a key it generated. That half is left to
.BR openconnect (8);
this program only separates authentication from connection.
.TP
.B token
The classic flow: the token arrives as a cookie on the gateway's own domain.
The
.B <config-auth>
XML protocol is spoken directly, and the cookie is collected as
.B \-\-capture
directs.
.TP
.B browserless
No browser at all. The identity provider's HTML forms are submitted directly
using
.B \-\-user
and a password. This works only where the login is plain HTML forms: a second
factor or a JavaScript-driven page ends it, with a message saying so. It never
resubmits a refused credential. Where a second factor is offered as a form,
.B \-\-factor
chooses between the options.
.IP
Unlike every other mode this one submits a password rather than a token, to
whatever form the walk reaches. So it refuses any hop that is not
.BR https ,
refuses to run with
.BR \-\-insecure ,
and prints the host that receives the password.
.TP
.B auto
Offers the external-browser method, and uses it when the gateway and the local
.BR openconnect (8)
both support it; otherwise falls back to
.BR token .
Never selects
.BR browserless ,
which is always explicit.
.
.SH TUNNEL GROUPS
On a Cisco gateway the tunnel group is selected by the URL the client posts to,
which becomes
.BR <group-access> .
Put the whole group URL in
.BR \-\-server .
The separate
.B <group-select>
element, which
.B \-\-group
sends, is widely ignored.
.B probe
prints the tunnel group the gateway actually resolved to, so a wrong URL path is
visible at a glance.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Read from
.I ~/.config/openconnect\-saml/config.toml
unless
.B $OPENCONNECT_SAML_CONFIG
names another file. Every top-level table is a profile; a top-level
.B default
key names the one to use when none is given on the command line. A file holding
exactly one profile needs no
.BR default .
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
default = "work"

[work]
server  = "https://vpn.example.com/saml"
browser = "chromium"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Recognised keys:
.BR server ", " group ", " browser ", " sudo ", " version ", " servercert ", "
.BR insecure ", " extra ", " user ", " passwordCommand ", " capture ", "
.BR cdpPort ", " browserProfile ", " captureTimeout .
Command-line options override the profile.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/openconnect\-saml/src/
The program: one module per topic, with
.I saml.j
as the entry point the executable imports by relative path. The executable in
.I /usr/bin
is a symlink to a thin wrapper beside that directory.
.TP
.I ~/.config/openconnect\-saml/config.toml
Profiles. Contains a gateway URL and possibly a certificate pin; keep it
private.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/openconnect\-saml/browser
Profile directory for the capture browser. Separate from your everyday browser
profile so the two never collide, and persistent so the identity provider's
session survives to the next connect.
.TP
.IR /run/openconnect\-saml\- <profile> .pid
The tunnel's process id, written by
.BR openconnect (8)
and read by
.BR disconnect .
.TP
.IR /run/openconnect\-saml\- <profile> .log
Output of the backgrounded
.BR openconnect (8).
Read this first when a tunnel drops or will not start.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPENCONNECT_SAML_CONFIG
Path to the configuration file, overriding the default location.
.TP
.B OPENCONNECT_SAML_PASSWORD
Password for
.B browserless
mode, used when
.B passwordCommand
is unset. Consulted before prompting.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR status ,
the tunnel is up.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed, or
.B status
found no tunnel.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown command, an unknown flag, or a bad option value.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Ask a gateway what it wants:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
openconnect\-saml probe \-\-server https://vpn.example.com/saml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Connect using the default profile, having primed
.BR sudo (8)
first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo \-v && openconnect\-saml connect
.EE
.RE
.PP
Connect without a browser, taking the password from a password store:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
openconnect\-saml connect \-\-mode browserless \-\-user jdoe \e
    \-\-password\-command "pass show vpn/work"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Authenticate only, and use the result yourself:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
eval "$(openconnect\-saml cookie)"
printf '%s\en' "$COOKIE" | sudo openconnect \-\-cookie\-on\-stdin \e
    \-\-servercert "$FINGERPRINT" "$CONNECT_URL"
.EE
.RE
.
.SH NOTES
.B connect
invokes the privilege helper non-interactively, because the child process has no
terminal on which to prompt. Run
.B sudo \-v
first, or use
.B \-\-print
to obtain the command and run it yourself.
.PP
The session cookie is passed to
.BR openconnect (8)
on standard input and never appears on a command line.
.PP
The certificate pin the gateway reports at the end of the login is passed to
.BR openconnect (8)
as
.BR \-\-servercert ,
so the tunnel is pinned to the certificate that authenticated the session.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openconnect (8),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR jennifer (1)
.PP
The README distributed with this program documents the protocol in more detail.
.
